ABSTRACT
Texas stands at the center of the American AI boom—and at the wall it has built. As of
August 2026, the Electric Reliability Council of Texas (ERCOT) is fielding approximately 474
GW of large-load interconnection requests, more than five times the grid's all-time record
peak demand of 91,089 MW, with roughly 90% originating from data centers. In response,
Governor Greg Abbott has ordered a full audit of the interconnection queue, paused new
large-load energization, and directed the Texas Commission on Environmental Quality to
stop issuing permits for new data centers until the audit is complete.
This paper argues that the Texas energy wall is not a grid-capacity problem. It is an
architecture problem. The workloads driving the queue are built on stochastic, unbounded
inference—a class of computation that cannot be specified, cannot be certified, and cannot
be bounded in energy or execution time. A deterministic alternative exists: ST-T1024, an
open, Apache-2.0-licensed standard for deterministic, agentic AI inference that runs at 3.7
μJ per query, with Assured Execution Time (AET) ≤ 800 ns, passive cooling, and air-gapped
sovereignty.
The paper proposes a four-path implementation plan for Texas: (1) a regulatory sandbox
pilot for independent silicon validation, (2) certification-first deployment in safety-critical
industries, (3) sovereign edge deployment for state agencies and critical infrastructure, and
(4) workforce development through the existing Texas CHIPS Act infrastructure. The claim is
not that ST-T1024 replaces stochastic AI. The claim is that a large fraction of the inference
workloads currently queued for ERCOT interconnection can be served without a grid
connection at all—bypassing the energy wall rather than climbing it.
1. THE PROBLEM: THE TEXAS ENERGY WALL
1.1 The Queue
In August 2026, Governor Abbott disclosed that ERCOT's large-load interconnection queue
had reached 474 GW of requested capacity—more than five times the grid's record peak of
91,089 MW set on July 22, 2026. Approximately 87–90% of those requests are from data
centers. ERCOT's own May 2026 forecast indicated that peak demand could more than
quadruple by 2032.


The gap between proposal and reality is stark. As of mid-2026, over 438 GW of proposed
large loads were primarily associated with data centers, but only a small fraction had
achieved energization. The queue is not a pipeline. It is a speculative reserve—developers
submitting multiple applications for the same project, or reserving capacity they may never
use.
1.2 The Political Response
Governor Abbott's response has been decisive and escalating:
· August 3, 2026: Abbott directed the Public Utility Commission of Texas (PUCT) and
ERCOT to conduct a comprehensive verification and audit of all data centers advancing
through the interconnection process, pausing Batch Zero classification.
· September 21, 2026: Abbott broadened the moratorium, directing the Texas Commission
on Environmental Quality (TCEQ) to stop issuing new air and water permits to data centers
until the audit is complete. The effect is a statewide halt on new data center construction
until at least December 2026.
The political incentive is clear. A New York Times-Siena poll found 61% of likely voters
opposed building data centers to power AI. A University of Texas poll found only 30% of
Texans supported data center construction in their own communities. Data centers have
become a liability in the governor's race, with Abbott's Democratic opponent calling for a
legislative moratorium.
1.3 The Architectural Root
The energy wall is not a capacity problem. It is an architecture problem.
The workloads filling the ERCOT queue are built on stochastic transformer inference—a
class of computation that:
· Produces a different output for the same input on different runs
· Cannot be specified in a form a verifier can test against
· Cannot be certified under any functional safety standard in force (ISO 26262, IEC 61508,
DO-178C, IEC 62443)
· Consumes energy scaling with search space, not with task relevance
· Requires active cooling and grid-scale power delivery
A system with no specification can never be wrong. It can only be "still scaling." The queue
is the physical manifestation of that logic: more capacity requested, no boundary declared,
no point at which the space collapses into something verifiable.
The question Texas faces is not "how do we power all of this?" It is "which of these
workloads actually require an unbounded architecture—and which can be served by a
bounded one?"
