3. WHY TEXAS IS THE RIGHT FIRST MOVER
3.1 The Problem Is Acute and Documented Texas is the epicenter of the American AI energy crunch. The 474 GW queue, the Abbott
moratorium, the public backlash—all of it is documented, timestamped, and politically
salient. The problem is not hypothetical. It is a governance crisis.
3.2 The Political Will Exists
Governor Abbott has staked his political capital on solving the data center problem. He has
ordered audits, paused permits, and directed agencies to ensure data centers pay their own
costs and do not use water needed by local communities. A solution that removes workloads
from the grid entirely—rather than merely managing their demand—directly serves the
governor's stated objectives.
3.3 The Infrastructure Already Exists
Texas has built the semiconductor and workforce infrastructure that ST-T1024 deployment
would require:
· Texas CHIPS Act (2023): $1.5 billion investment, including $698.3 million for the Texas
Semiconductor Innovation Fund (TSIF).
· Texas Semiconductor Innovation Consortium (TSIC): Statewide coordination of
semiconductor research, design, and manufacturing.
· Texas Institute for Electronics (TIE) at UT Austin: Awarded $840 million DARPA Next
Generation Microelectronics Manufacturing program, focused on 3D heterogeneous
integration.
· Texas A&M University: $200 million semiconductor workforce development project,
breaking ground in 2026.
· Arm Austin Engineering Hub: $71 million expansion, creating 320+ skilled jobs in AI-driven
chip design.
· Texas Instruments Hercules TMS570: Already certified by TÜV SÜD to IEC 61508 SIL 3
and ISO 26262 ASIL D—a Texas-built platform that could serve as the hardware substrate
for ST-T1024 implementation.
3.4 The Regulatory Sandbox Exists
Texas HB 149, the Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act, establishes an AI
Regulatory Sandbox Program to encourage innovation under controlled conditions. The
Department of Information Resources (DIR) is authorized to establish an AI division and
assist state agencies in AI implementation. The Texas Artificial Intelligence Council (TAIC) is
established to conduct training programs for state agencies. The institutional mechanism for
a pilot already exists.
