Path Two: Certification-First Industrial Deployment
Objective: Establish ST-T1024 as the first certified deterministic AI in safety-critical markets
where stochastic AI is structurally excluded.
Mechanism: Partner with a Texas-based tier-1 industrial or automotive supplier to certify
ST-T1024 for a specific safety-critical application.
Why Texas:
· Texas Instruments already builds IEC 61508 SIL 3 / ISO 26262 ASIL D certified
microcontrollers (Hercules TMS570 family) in Texas.
· TIE at UT Austin has the advanced packaging capability for custom silicon.
· Texas's industrial base—energy, manufacturing, logistics, aerospace—has certification
requirements that cannot be met by cloud AI.
Target Certification: IEC 61508 SIL 2 (achievable in 12 months), with a path to SIL 3.
Deliverable: First certified ST-T1024 implementation, with a public safety case.
Path Three: Sovereign Edge Deployment
Objective: Deploy ST-T1024 as sovereign, air-gapped AI infrastructure for Texas state
agencies and critical infrastructure.
Mechanism: Position ST-T1024 as the infrastructure for Texas AI sovereignty—data stays
local, chips don't come from a foreign vendor, models don't phone home.
Candidate Applications: · ERCOT: Air-gapped, deterministic grid management agents for real-time stability
assessment
· Texas Cyber Command: Deterministic threat classification and response orchestration (IEC
62443 SL 3 ready)
· State agencies: Deterministic document processing, benefits eligibility, and regulatory
compliance under HB 149
Why This Works: Cloud AI is structurally dependent on foreign infrastructure. ST-T1024 is
air-gapped by design. For a state that has already moved to restrict foreign technology in
critical infrastructure, this is not a preference—it is a requirement.
Path Four: Workforce Development
Objective: Produce the first generation of deterministic AI engineers.
Mechanism: Leverage the existing Texas CHIPS Act infrastructure—TSIC, TSIF, TIE, and
Texas A&M's semiconductor workforce programs—to establish ST-T1024 engineering
curricula at Texas institutions.
Curriculum:
· 5-year mainstream embedded systems track with ST-T1024 specialization
· A-la-carte addendum for practicing engineers
· Certification preparation for IEC 61508, ISO 26262, DO-178C
Why Texas:
· Austin Community College already has nationally recognized semiconductor technician
training programs.
· Texas A&M's ESET Program has established embedded systems and space-qualified PCB
design curricula.
· UT Austin's TIE has the research infrastructure for advanced packaging and 3DHI.
Deliverable: First cohort of ST-T1024-certified engineers by 2028.
