6. CONCLUSION: BYPASS, NOT CLIMB
The Texas energy wall is real. 474 GW of requests against a 91 GW record peak is not a
manageable queue—it is a structural crisis. Governor Abbott's response—audit, pause,
permit freeze—is the correct first move. But an audit cannot solve an architecture problem. It
can only reveal it.
ST-T1024 offers a different path. Not "how do we power all of this?" but "which of these
workloads actually require an unbounded architecture—and which can be served by a
bounded one?"
The answer, for a large fraction of inference workloads, is that they can be served at 3.7 μJ
per query, with passive cooling, no water consumption, and air-gapped sovereignty—without
a grid connection at all.
The specification is complete. The formal verification is done. The agentic protocol stack is
drafted. The four-norms alignment is documented. The ledger is honest: no silicon
measurement, no independent replication, no adopters.
Texas has the problem. Texas has the political will. Texas has the semiconductor
infrastructure. Texas has the regulatory sandbox. Texas has the workforce development
programs.
What Texas does not yet have is the first measured implementation.
That is the door. It is unlocked.
